    {
      "id": "hacker-news-03b179946dd0e551",
      "source": "arXiv.org",
      "topic": "AI 모델링",
      "title": "Language Models Can Rewrite Their Own Weights",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21885",
      "published_at": "2026-11-21T01:29:06+09:00",
      "summary": "A language model is trained once and frozen. Making it better takes another training run, or a prompt, a harness or extra computation at inference that is gone once the task ends. We show a third way: to our knowledge for the first time, a language model rewrites its own weights by its own reasoning and becomes better, with no gradients and no training. We call this self-rewriting: the model is its own optimizer. In earlier work, a frontier model edited a much smaller one; here an open 27B model, given its weights, its graded answers and the ability to run itself, diagnoses its own failure and rewrites the weights behind it. It surpasses itself: its own edit makes it 6.6 points more accurate on problems it never saw. It keeps getting better: it tests its edits on practice problems and submits only what works, each edit improving on the last, and over eight consecutive sessions its accuracy only rises. It repairs a damaged copy of itself, like a broken robot that fixes itself without being rebuilt: the copy solves almost nothing, yet with no retraining and without ever reading the original weights, the model brings it back to over 80% of the original accuracy on image tasks it never",
      "listing_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50577265",
      "introduced_at": "2026-11-21T01:29:06+09:00",
      "image_url": "https://arxiv.org/static/browse/0.3.4/images/arxiv-logo-fb.png",
